hey guys it's Jenna here it's Chinese  New Year in the next couple of days and  I can't wait to go back to my hometown  to see all my friends and family members  that's going to be a lot of food house  visiting and lots of catching up to do  the kisses Chinese New Year I'm going to  share with you three of my favorite  Asian foods if you have been watching my  videos you will know that I like simple  very quick recipes healthy very  flavoursome and eating them will make  you feel good  so today's recipes do not require any  cooking you don't have to turn the stove  on and you don't need an oven you can  serve them for breakfast lunch dinner or  even as a snack so let's start by making  my childhood favorite Malaysian crispy  seaweed prepare if you ask any of my  friends if I can't decide on what I want  to have I will always resort to having  crispy sweet opium I just love the  combination of fresh sweet turnips with  seaweed so it is something I've eaten  from a very young age and I can  literally have this every single day and  I'm still not bored of it so to make one  serving of papaya what you'll need are  one large spring roll wrap one large  seaweed sheet about half a cup of sweet  turnip  shredded about quarter cup of carrots  shredded 2 tablespoons of a sweet corn  and just about 1 tablespoon of peanuts  crushed so on a flat surface place one  sheet of sea-weed and one sheet of  spring roll wrap on top of the seaweed  then arrange a generous amount of sweet  turnips shredded carrots a little bit of  sweet corn and crushed peanuts while  traditionally they would add a little  bit of mayonnaise I like to add a little  bit of garlic chili sauce you can choose  to top it up with any sources of your  choice then press a very firmly I roll  it up just like making a sushi roll  use a sharp knife and cut the spring  roll wrap into eight equal portions  serve fresh everybody you take your bite  into the sweetness and crunchiness of  the turnips carrots and corn combined  with the taste of seaweed it is just  absolutely delicious so if you're not a  vegan you can also choose to add meat  floss bonito flakes tuna eggs or crab  stick into the wrap H a serving is only  about 153 calories and I love to have  this for my breakfast it is best to be  eaten fresh so what you can do is prep  the vegetables ahead and only put them  together and roll them up when ready to  serve the next recipe is also another  favorite of mine when I visit a  Vietnamese or Thai restaurant cold glass  noodles salads I love glass noodles but  it is not commonly served so if you were  to dine in a Vietnamese or Thai  restaurant you might find this dish on  their menu so this is what it looks like  uncooked it is also known as moonbeam  noodles or cellophane noodles I really  enjoy the silky and fine texture of the  noodles so the noodles on their own do  not have a very prominent flavor but  rather it takes on the flavors of other  ingredients added into the dish and the  best part is it doesn't require any  cooking so to make this salad what  you'll need are five servings of glass  noodles about half a medium cabbage  shredded 2 medium carrots peeled and  shredded one red bell pepper diced one  Japanese cucumber thinly sliced half a  cup of fresh basil costly chopped half a  cup of fresh coriander costly chopped  one large spring onion thinly sliced and  about half a cup of unsalted peanuts  crushed and of course we'll also need to  make the spicy lime vinaigrette dressing  to go along with the salad so what  you'll need are three tablespoons of  fresh lime juice three tablespoons of  rice wine vinegar 1 tablespoon of soy  sauce 1/2 a tablespoon of sesame oil 1/2  a tbsp of olive oil  about half a tablespoon of maple syrup  one garlic clove minced and about half a  teaspoon of dried chili flakes so let's  go ahead and prep the noodles fill up a  large bowl with boiling hot water then  just soak the glass noodles for about 10  to 15 minutes until soft be sure to  cover the bowl now let's go ahead and  prep the salad dressing so in a small  bowl combine all the ingredients mix  well and set aside now let's go ahead  and prep the salad in a very large bowl  combine all the chopped up vegetables  and herbs stir in the dressing and toss  until well combined and allow the salad  mixture to sit for at least 10 minutes  for the vegetables to fully absorb the  flavors of the dressing let's check on  the glass noodles drain off the water  shake and they should be done to serve  place the glass noodles on a plate then  top it up with a generous amount of  salad  and finally just sprinkle with a little  bit of peanuts so what I really like  about this dish is that you can prep the  salad mixture ahead and in fact it would  taste better the next day the vegetables  will fully absorb the flavors of the  dressing and herbs to eat it just toss  the salad with the glass noodles the  noodles would take on the flavors of the  salad mixture and add a nice silky  texture to the dish this is a really  great work lunch you can just bring the  salad mixture and chill it in the fridge  at work and when you want to eat all you  have to do is soak the glass noodles in  hot boiling water and surf I also like  to serve this as a starter or snack  without the glass noodles so you have to  try it out and the final and easiest  dish is here yaku also known as Japanese  chilled tofu this dish is fresh and cold  and perfect for the hot summer which is  pretty much 24/7 all year round in  Malaysia it is ridiculous  the easy and quick to put together it  takes less than five minutes what you'll  need are one block of silken or soft  tofu drained and washed about one spring  onion thinly sliced one tablespoon of  grated ginger one to two tablespoons of  soy sauce and bonito flakes if you're a  vegan just omit the bonito flakes so  let's start by prepping the tofu place  it on a dish and carefully cut into six  equal slices then top it up with grated  ginger and a generous amount of spring  onions if you're not a vegan you can  also choose to top it up with the bonito  flakes which is how I like to have it  and finally drizzle some soy sauce over  the tofu and serve chilled see I told  you it is really quick and simple here  yaku is usually served as a side dish or  starter the tofu will take on the  flavors of the spring onions bonito  flakes and also ginger it is really  light and refreshing so guys if there  are days where you just feel lazy you  don't feel like cooking well make these  three Asian dishes they're are so simple  so quick to put together they are yummy  and I can assure you you will feel good  eating them so be sure to like and share  this video make them and let me know  which is your favorite in the comments  below  go see fat choy
 

